---js
{
  layout: "layouts/base.webc",
  meta: {
    title: "Targets",
    description: "Every DynamoDB target the conformance suite tracks, with its current score and a link to its full history.",
  },
  // The cards show each target's latest-run score, so freshness tracks the data,
  // not this file's git date. Keeps the sitemap <lastmod> honest.
  eleventyComputed: {
    lastmod: (data) => data.conformance?.latest?.date,
  },
}
---
<section class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-zinc-900 dark:text-white mb-2">Targets</h1>
  <p class="text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 mb-8 max-w-2xl">
    Every target the suite tracks, with its score from the latest run. Open one to see its tier breakdown and how its conformance has moved over time.
  </p>
  <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
    <!-- One card per project. A build of one is named on its parent's card
         instead of taking a card beside it, so the page does not read as though
         a project shipping two builds is two engines.
         Which row that is comes from the row itself, not its slug: when the
         reference build has no result the grouping promotes another build to
         stand for the project, and reading the slug dropped the reference card
         (no current row) and the promoted one (a build) both, losing the
         project from this page. The slug stays as the reading for a model
         restored from the committed fallback, which predates the flag. -->
    <target-card
      webc:for="slug of $data.conformance.targets"
      webc:if="$data.conformance.perTarget[slug].current && standsForProject($data.conformance.perTarget[slug].current)"
      :row="$data.conformance.perTarget[slug].current"></target-card>
  </div>

  <suggest-target></suggest-target>
</section>
